1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,864
n8n is open source, and that one fact drives every choice in this lecture.

2
00:00:04,864 --> 00:00:09,926
It means you can run it yourself, on your own computer, for as long as you like, at

3
00:00:09,926 --> 00:00:11,573
no cost beyond electricity.

4
00:00:11,573 --> 00:00:16,331
It also means n8n sells a hosted version for people who would rather not run a

5
00:00:16,331 --> 00:00:16,758
server.

6
00:00:16,758 --> 00:00:18,160
Both are the same tool.

7
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:21,820
The difference is who is responsible for keeping it running.

8
00:00:21,820 --> 00:00:25,053
What does "run it yourself" actually involve, though?

9
00:00:25,053 --> 00:00:29,810
Less than the phrase suggests, and it is worth demystifying before you choose.

10
00:00:29,810 --> 00:00:34,568
Self-hosted n8n is one small program running quietly in the background of your

11
00:00:34,568 --> 00:00:39,692
computer, and your browser is the whole interface: you open a tab, and the canvas is

12
00:00:39,692 --> 00:00:40,058
there.

13
00:00:40,058 --> 00:00:42,497
Nothing else changes about your machine.

14
00:00:42,497 --> 00:00:46,096
The honest cost is the one word inside that sentence: your.

15
00:00:46,096 --> 00:00:50,244
Your agents run while your computer is on, and pause when it is not.

16
00:00:50,244 --> 00:00:55,123
For a work laptop and office hours tasks, which is everything Priya builds, that

17
00:00:55,123 --> 00:00:56,221
costs you nothing.

18
00:00:56,221 --> 00:01:01,345
The day you want an agent working at three in the morning while the laptop sleeps in

19
00:01:01,345 --> 00:01:06,407
a drawer, that is the day the hosted conversation genuinely starts, and not before.

20
00:01:06,957 --> 00:01:11,962
Cloud's higher tiers exist for teams running thousands of workflow runs a month.

21
00:01:11,962 --> 00:01:16,341
Section 10's rollout plan is where that conversation becomes relevant.

22
00:01:16,341 --> 00:01:21,346
For everything in this course, self-hosted is not a compromise, it is the better

23
00:01:21,346 --> 00:01:24,161
choice: unlimited runs, and nothing to renew.

24
00:01:24,161 --> 00:01:27,790
Do the execution arithmetic before the cap looks generous.

25
00:01:27,790 --> 00:01:32,732
One run is one pass through a workflow, and a triage agent runs once per email:

26
00:01:32,732 --> 00:01:37,862
Priya's inbox alone burns roughly nine hundred runs a month before her other three

27
00:01:37,862 --> 00:01:39,050
agents do anything.

28
00:01:39,050 --> 00:01:42,053
One busy agent, a third of the Starter cap gone.

29
00:01:42,053 --> 00:01:47,308
That is the quiet trap in per-run pricing everywhere, not just here: the agents that

30
00:01:47,308 --> 00:01:52,500
help most are the ones that run most, so success is exactly what walks you into the

31
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:53,001
ceiling.

32
00:01:53,001 --> 00:01:58,193
Unlimited runs on the self-hosted side is not a nice-to-have for a course that ends

33
00:01:58,193 --> 00:02:00,133
with four agents running daily.

34
00:02:00,133 --> 00:02:05,137
It is the difference between adding your next agent freely and auditing your old

35
00:02:05,137 --> 00:02:06,764
ones for permission first.

36
00:02:07,314 --> 00:02:12,507
Every screenshot in this course was captured against a self-hosted instance, so what

37
00:02:12,507 --> 00:02:15,784
you see on screen matches what you will see on yours.

38
00:02:15,784 --> 00:02:20,978
If your workplace already runs n8n on a server somewhere, ask whoever manages it for

39
00:02:20,978 --> 00:02:23,574
an account instead of installing your own.

40
00:02:23,574 --> 00:02:25,120
Same tool, same lectures.

41
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:30,128
What the ten minutes of setup actually contains: n8n's install page gives you one

42
00:02:30,128 --> 00:02:35,198
command to paste into a terminal, and that command needs one prerequisite the same

43
00:02:35,198 --> 00:02:36,063
page links to.

44
00:02:36,063 --> 00:02:41,133
That is the whole recipe, and the reason this course does not walk it keystroke by

45
00:02:41,133 --> 00:02:45,770
keystroke is that the page moves with each release and this video does not.

46
00:02:45,770 --> 00:02:49,356
Follow their current page, not a screenshot of an old one.

47
00:02:49,906 --> 00:02:54,902
A tool nobody maintains is how workplace automation quietly dies, and it is a five

48
00:02:54,902 --> 00:02:56,242
minute habit to avoid.

49
00:02:56,242 --> 00:03:01,116
Put a monthly reminder in your calendar now, while this lecture is open, and the

50
00:03:01,116 --> 00:03:03,797
habit exists before it has ever been needed.

51
00:03:03,797 --> 00:03:08,488
Future you does not get a say in whether past you set that reminder, which is

52
00:03:08,488 --> 00:03:09,767
exactly why it works.

53
00:03:10,317 --> 00:03:15,105
One practical note about that form: the password it asks for is a new one you are

54
00:03:15,105 --> 00:03:19,834
inventing for this install, not your email password or anything you already use.

55
00:03:19,834 --> 00:03:24,799
It guards the door to your workflows on this machine, and since those workflows will

56
00:03:24,799 --> 00:03:29,351
eventually hold saved credentials to your real accounts, treat it like a work

57
00:03:29,351 --> 00:03:31,183
password, not like a wifi code.

58
00:03:31,183 --> 00:03:36,149
If the form ever asks you something this lecture did not mention, the version moved.

59
00:03:36,149 --> 00:03:37,035
Trust the form.

60
00:03:37,035 --> 00:03:41,882
And write the password somewhere real, a password manager if you have one, because

61
00:03:41,882 --> 00:03:46,316
this is the login you will use every working day from here to the capstone.

62
00:03:46,316 --> 00:03:50,926
Fill it in, and you land on an empty canvas with one button: build a workflow.

63
00:03:50,926 --> 00:03:53,763
You have seen that screen already, in Section 0.

64
00:03:53,763 --> 00:03:58,729
Before you move on, run the thirty second victory lap: close the browser tab, open a

65
00:03:58,729 --> 00:04:00,857
new one, and find your canvas again.

66
00:04:00,857 --> 00:04:01,743
Sounds trivial.

67
00:04:01,743 --> 00:04:06,531
It is the step that proves to you the tool lives on your machine and not in a tab

68
00:04:06,531 --> 00:04:11,319
you are afraid to lose, and everyone skips it and then messages someone in a mild

69
00:04:11,319 --> 00:04:12,561
panic two days later.

70
00:04:12,561 --> 00:04:17,408
Next lecture, we connect the other half of the toolbox: the AI model that does the

71
00:04:17,408 --> 00:04:19,063
actual reading and deciding.
